  "content": "<img src=\"img/question-tags-rules-ssc-cgl-bank-po.webp\" alt=\"Question Tags Rules for SSC CGL and Bank PO\" style=\"width:100%;border-radius:8px;\" />\n\n<h2>You Know the Basic Rule. You Still Mark the Wrong Tag.</h2>\n<p>You remember “positive statement gets negative tag”. Then the exam throws “I am”, “Let’s”, “Nobody”, or “hardly” and the marks disappear. <strong>Question tags</strong> look simple until the sentence mixes polarity, tense and special cases in one line. In the next few minutes you will see the exact patterns that cost marks in SSC CGL and Bank PO, and the fixed checks that stop the loss.</p>\n\n<div style=\"background:#f0f7ff;border-left:4px solid #1B3A6B;padding:15px 18px;margin:20px 0;border-radius:4px;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\"><strong>Quick Answer:</strong> A question tag repeats the auxiliary of the main clause, uses the opposite polarity, and matches the subject with the correct pronoun. Special fixed forms — aren’t I, shall we, didn’t for used to, and they for nobody/everybody — appear every year. Master the polarity rule plus these five exceptions and most tag errors disappear.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Table of Contents</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"#the-problem\">Why Question Tags Keep Costing Marks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#common-mistakes\">5 Question Tag Mistakes Aspirants Repeat</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#the-solution\">6-Step Strategy to Form Perfect Tags</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#case-study\">How One Student Stopped Losing Marks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#expert-tips\">Expert Tips From 15+ Years of Coaching</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#faqs\">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-problem\">Why Question Tags Keep Costing Marks</h2>\n<p>Error-spotting and sentence-improvement questions on tags are never about the basic rule. They test whether you notice the hidden negative word, the special subject, or the wrong auxiliary under time pressure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You apply the polarity rule but miss “hardly / never / seldom” which already make the statement negative.</li>\n<li>You write “amn’t I” or “isn’t I” instead of the fixed “aren’t I”.</li>\n<li>You keep the same polarity when the sentence starts with “Let’s” or “There”.</li>\n<li>You match the verb but forget the pronoun must be they for nobody, everybody, someone.</li>\n<li>You panic and change a correct tag because the sentence “sounds” odd in spoken Indian English.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I know exactly how that feels. Students leave the exam hall replaying the tag and realising the mistake thirty seconds too late. But here’s what most people get wrong: they keep memorising more examples instead of locking the five fixed exceptions.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">5 Question Tag Mistakes Aspirants Repeat Every Year</h2>\n\n<p><strong>1. Same polarity instead of opposite polarity</strong><br>\nPositive statement needs negative tag. Negative statement needs positive tag. Exams hide this in long sentences.<br>\nWrong: Your eldest sister lives in a big city, does she?<br>\nCorrect: Your eldest sister lives in a big city, doesn’t she?<br>\n<em>Source: SSC CGL (Tier-I) 13/04/2022 Shift-I</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Wrong tag for simple present or past when no auxiliary is visible</strong><br>\nWhen the main verb is in simple present or past, the tag uses do/does/did.<br>\nWrong: Every Saturday, your mother prepares a pudding, isn’t she?<br>\nCorrect: Every Saturday, your mother prepares a pudding, doesn’t she?<br>\n<em>Source: SSC CGL (Tier-II) 08.08.2022 Shift-II</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>3. Using “isn’t it” for every “should / can / will” sentence</strong><br>\nThe tag must repeat the same modal or auxiliary.<br>\nWrong: The education of girls is a primary concern… so it should be started early, isn’t it?<br>\nCorrect: …shouldn’t it?<br>\n<em>Source: SSC Constable GD 17/11/2021 Shift-II</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>4. “I am” → amn’t I or isn’t I</strong><br>\nThe only accepted form is aren’t I.<br>\nWrong: I am late, amn’t I? / isn’t I?<br>\nCorrect: I am late, aren’t I?</p>\n\n<p><strong>5. “Let’s” and “Nobody / Everybody” traps</strong><br>\nLet’s always takes shall we. Nobody / everybody / someone take they.<br>\nWrong: Let’s start the meeting, will we? / Nobody came, did he?<br>\nCorrect: Let’s start the meeting, shall we? / Nobody came, did they?</p>\n\n<div style=\"max-width:700px;margin:30px auto;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;\">\n  <h3 style=\"background:#1B3A6B;color:#F5A623;padding:14px 20px;border-radius:8px 8px 0 0;margin:0;font-size:18px;text-align:center;\">\n    Common Mistakes Students Make\n  </h3>\n  <div style=\"border:2px solid #1B3A6B;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;overflow:hidden;\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 1: Same polarity</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Positive statement must take negative tag.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: Your eldest sister lives in a big city, does she?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: doesn’t she?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 2: Wrong auxiliary for simple present</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Use does/doesn’t when no helping verb is present.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: …prepares a pudding, isn’t she?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: doesn’t she?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 3: “isn’t it” for every modal</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Tag must repeat the same modal.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: …should be started early, isn’t it?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: shouldn’t it?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 4: “I am” tag</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Only “aren’t I” is accepted.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: I am late, amn’t I?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: aren’t I?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 5: “Let’s” and “Nobody”</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Let’s → shall we; Nobody → they.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: Let’s go, will we? / Nobody came, did he?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: shall we? / did they?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#888;margin-top:8px;\">ebookcharm — English Grammar Made Exam-Ready</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-solution\">6-Step Strategy to Form Perfect Tags</h2>\n<p>You do not need twenty rules. You need a fixed sequence you can run in under twenty seconds.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: Decide polarity first.</strong> Look for negative words (not, never, hardly, seldom, rarely, no one, nobody). If any of these is present the statement is already negative → positive tag. Otherwise negative tag.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 2: Find the auxiliary or modal in the main clause.</strong> is/are/was/were/have/has/had/will/would/can/could/should/must → repeat it in the tag. If no auxiliary is visible, use do/does/did according to tense and person.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 3: Match the subject with the correct pronoun.</strong> He/she/it → he/she/it. They/we/you → they/we/you. There → there. Nobody/everybody/someone/anyone → they. I → I (but “I am” becomes aren’t I).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 4: Lock the five fixed exceptions.</strong><br>\n• I am → aren’t I<br>\n• Let’s → shall we<br>\n• Used to → didn’t<br>\n• There is/are → isn’t/aren’t there<br>\n• Imperative (Open the door) → will you / won’t you</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 5: Never put the main verb in the tag.</strong> The tag contains only auxiliary + pronoun + ?</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 6: Read the whole sentence once before choosing the option.</strong> Many aspirants lock onto the first odd word and miss a polarity error later.</p>\n\n<div style=\"max-width:700px;margin:30px auto;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;\">\n  <h3 style=\"background:#1B3A6B;color:#F5A623;padding:14px 20px;border-radius:8px 8px 0 0;margin:0;font-size:18px;text-align:center;\">\n    Step-by-Step Strategy\n  </h3>\n  <div style=\"border:2px solid #1B3A6B;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;padding:10px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">1</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Decide polarity first</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Look for never, hardly, seldom, nobody → positive tag.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: He never comes late, does he?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">2</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Find the auxiliary or use do/does/did</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">No helping verb → do/does/did according to tense.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: She writes novels, doesn’t she?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">3</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Match the correct pronoun</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Nobody / everybody / someone → they.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: Nobody came, did they?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">4</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Lock the five fixed exceptions</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">I am → aren’t I; Let’s → shall we; used to → didn’t.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: I am ready, aren’t I?</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">5</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Never put the main verb in the tag</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Only auxiliary + pronoun + ?</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: He runs fast, doesn’t he? (not doesn’t he run)</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">6</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Read the full sentence once</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Don’t lock onto the first odd word.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: Re-read the whole sentence silently before marking.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#888;margin-top:8px;\">ebookcharm — English Grammar Made Exam-Ready</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"case-study\">How One Student Stopped Losing Marks</h2>\n<p>Rahul, an SSC CGL aspirant from Pune, was losing two to three marks on every mock purely on question-tag questions. He knew the basic polarity rule but froze on “I am”, “Let’s” and “nobody”. For three weeks he wrote only the five fixed exceptions on a flashcard and reviewed them before every mock. Tag-related errors dropped to zero. Nothing else in his preparation changed — just the shift from trying to remember every possible sentence to locking the exceptions. You can do the same — here’s how to start.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"expert-tips\">Expert Tips From 15+ Years of Coaching</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Underline every negative word first.</strong> Hardly, never, seldom, rarely, no, nobody already decide the polarity for you.</li>\n<li><strong>Treat “I am” and “Let’s” as fixed formulas, not rules you calculate.</strong> The moment you see them, write aren’t I or shall we without thinking.</li>\n<li><strong>Practise only real exam sentences, not textbook ones.</strong> Short made-up sentences build false confidence. The real exam hides the tag inside longer clauses.</li>\n<li><strong>Never trust how the sentence sounds in spoken Indian English.</strong> “isn’t it” is used for everything in speech; the exam does not accept it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">The Bottom Line</h2>\n<p>Question tags stop costing marks the moment you stop treating them as a list of twenty rules and start running the six-step sequence: polarity → auxiliary → pronoun → fixed exceptions → no main verb → full-sentence check. Master those five exceptions and the polarity rule, and the topic becomes one of the most reliable mark-scoring areas in SSC CGL and Bank PO.</p>\n\n<div style=\"background:#fff8e1;border:2px solid #F5A623;padding:20px;border-radius:8px;text-align:center;margin:30px 0;\">\n<img src=\"img/question-tags-zero-errors-cover.webp\" alt=\"Question Tags : Zero Errors by Balu Kandekar\" style=\"max-width:200px;margin-bottom:15px;\" />\n<h3>Stop Losing Marks on Question Tags</h3>\n<p><strong>Question Tags : Zero Errors</strong> — 186 Rules, 60 Traps & 200+ MCQs for SSC, IBPS & Railways — gives you every exam-relevant pattern with real practice sets for just $2.99.</p>\n<a href=\"https://amzn.to/4gvkRrf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"background:#1B3A6B;color:#fff;padding:12px 24px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:5px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;\">Grab Your Copy on Amazon ($2.99) →</a>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"faqs\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: What is the most common question tag error in SSC CGL?</h4>\n<p>A: Using the same polarity (positive tag after positive statement) and wrong tag for simple present/past verbs (isn’t she instead of doesn’t she).</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Is “aren’t I” the only correct tag for “I am”?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes. “Amn’t I” and “isn’t I” are always wrong in standard exam English.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: What tag is used after “Let’s”?</h4>\n<p>A: Always “shall we”. “will we” or “won’t we” is incorrect.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Do words like “hardly” and “never” change the tag?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes. They make the statement negative, so the tag must be positive.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Is this eBook useful if I already know basic question tag rules?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes. It focuses on the exact traps and real SSC/Bank patterns that basic textbooks skip.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Will these rules work for IBPS PO as well?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes. Question-tag patterns overlap heavily across SSC CGL, IBPS PO, RRB and similar exams.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Related Posts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Subject-Verb Agreement Rules for SSC CGL</a> — the other high-frequency error area</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Tense Errors in SSC CGL & Bank PO</a> — fix the time-marker traps</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Direct and Indirect Speech: Complete Guide</a> — master the backshift rule</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Active vs Passive Voice Made Simple</a> — spot voice errors fast</li>\n</ul>"
